\subsection{OOP CONCEPTS COVERAGE}
 
\textbf{ENCAPSULATION:}\\
\texttt{Player} and \texttt{Zombie} keep their attributes (health, hunger, bullets, food, damage) private, exposing controlled behaviour only through public methods such as \texttt{eat()}, \texttt{damage()}, \texttt{shoot()}, and \texttt{attack()}.
 
\textbf{COMPOSITION:}\\
The \texttt{Game} class owns a \texttt{Player} object for the lifetime of a session and creates \texttt{Zombie} objects on demand whenever a combat encounter occurs, modelling a clear ``has-a'' relationship between classes.
 
\textbf{CONSTRUCTORS:}\\
Each class defines a constructor that establishes its starting state: \texttt{Player} initializes health, hunger, bullets, and food to their starting values from the player's name; \texttt{Zombie} assigns a randomized damage value on creation; \texttt{Game} initializes the day counter and constructs its \texttt{Player}.
 
\textbf{CONTROL FLOW AND STATE MANAGEMENT:}\\
The \texttt{Game::start()} and \texttt{Game::fightZombie()} methods manage nested loops and conditional branching to drive the turn-based menu, the combat loop, and the end-of-game detection, reinforcing state-management and defensive-programming practice.
 
